# /etc/talyvor/bff.env — the BFF's entire runtime surface.
#   chown root:talyvor /etc/talyvor/bff.env && chmod 640 /etc/talyvor/bff.env
# (0640, not 0600: systemd reads this as root, but the preflight boot — and any
# as-talyvor debugging — sources it AS THE SERVICE USER; group-read makes that
# possible while everyone else is kept out. See deploy/README.md §4.)
# Every value here is documented, with its failure mode, in deploy/README.md §4.
# The BFF fails closed: a missing required value refuses to start and names itself.

# ── Bind & mode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 0.0.0.0, NOT loopback, on this server: Caddy is a CONTAINER (the Lens stack's
# compose), and from inside it 127.0.0.1 is the container — it reaches the host
# only over the Docker bridge, so a loopback-bound BFF is unreachable ("dial
# tcp 172.17.0.1:8787: i/o timeout"). The bind guard permits this ONLY because
# BFF_AUTH_MODE=oidc with an https BFF_PUBLIC_BASE_URL; ufw scopes :8787 to the
# bridge range (172.16.0.0/12 — README §3a). For loopback dev, 127.0.0.1:8787.
BFF_ADDR=0.0.0.0:8787
# Production posture. ("disabled" is the loopback-only dev mode.)
BFF_AUTH_MODE=oidc

# ── Web bundle ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the built SPA lives on the server. REQUIRED here in practice: the
# compiled-in default (../web/dist) is a repo-relative dev path that means
# nothing under systemd, and the unit deliberately does not set it.
WEB_DIST=/opt/talyvor/web-dist

# ── OIDC (all five required in oidc mode) ────────────────────────────────────
# The browser-facing origin. Bare origin, https, no path — it derives the
# redirect URI (<origin>/auth/callback) and scopes the __Host- session cookie.
BFF_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://app.talyvor.com
# The issuer. Google (recommended for the trial): https://accounts.google.com
# The BFF discovers it at boot; the BROWSER must be able to reach it too.
OIDC_ISSUER=https://accounts.google.com
# The confidential client registered at the IdP, with redirect URI EXACTLY
# https://app.talyvor.com/auth/callback
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_ME.apps.googleusercontent.com
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME
# The phone app's client ids at the same issuer (its iOS and Android clients),
# comma-separated. The phone sends their ID token to POST /auth/mobile/session
# and gets a Bearer session back. Empty → that route refuses; the browser is unaffected.
OIDC_MOBILE_CLIENT_IDS=
# ⚠ THE ONLY THING GATING SIGNUP. Google does NOT gate it for us: with only the
# openid/email/profile scopes this app requests, any Google account completes the
# Google half regardless of publishing status or test-user list. Whatever is on
# this line is the whole lock. Read at BOOT — changing it needs a BFF restart.
#
#   PUBLIC TRIAL  → *              anyone who authenticates gets their OWN empty
#                                  workspace; the only writers into an LXC balance
#                                  are the Stripe top-up and a default-off admin
#                                  grant route, so an open door costs no money.
#   CLOSED TRIAL  → the list       comma-separated, matched lower-cased against the
#                                  id_token email; unverified emails are refused.
#                                  Price: every new tester waits for an edit AND a
#                                  restart. Refusals are logged with the address.
#
# Uncomment ONE. Empty → the BFF refuses to start (authorization must be stated).
#OIDC_ALLOWED_EMAILS=*
OIDC_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@example.com,teammate@example.com
# Optional; default 12h. Sessions are in-memory: a BFF restart signs everyone out.
#BFF_SESSION_TTL=12h
# Optional. Postgres that keeps assistant sign-ins (Talyvor Connect at /mcp) across
# restarts; the BFF creates its oauth_* tables at boot. Unset: they are in memory and
# every restart signs every connected assistant out.
#BFF_DATABASE_URL=postgres://bff:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5432/bff?sslmode=disable
#
# ⚠ WHO MAY SEE EVERYTHING — a DIFFERENT question from who may sign in, and this
# file did not name it at all until now. Comma-separated OIDC `sub` values (NOT
# emails) allowed to read EVERY tenant's spend, purchases and royalties.
# UNSET MEANS NOBODY, INCLUDING YOU — a fresh deployment has no operator until
# someone is named here, and there is deliberately no `*`. `sub` is opaque, so
# sign in once and read yours out of /auth/me. Read at boot; changing it needs a
# restart. Leave commented for a deployment with no operator screens.
#OPERATOR_SUBS=110248495000000000000

# ── Lens (LENS_BASE_URL + LENS_PROVISION_SECRET required, always) ────────────
# https for a remote Lens box — ENFORCED at boot (credentials ride every request
# to it; http is allowed only on loopback, for dev).
LENS_BASE_URL=https://REPLACE_ME_LENS_HOST
#
# ⚠ HOW A CUSTOMER REACHES LENS — and this file did not name it until now, which
# is why the Setup page was dark on deployments built from it. This is the origin
# your users put in OPENAI_BASE_URL / ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, so it must resolve FROM
# THEIR MACHINE. It is NOT LENS_BASE_URL: that one may be a loopback or
# compose-internal address, and printing it would hand every trial user a URL that
# cannot resolve.
#
# NO DEFAULT, on purpose. Unset ⇒ /api/context serves "" and the Setup page says
# "Setup instructions unavailable — ask your operator to set LENS_PUBLIC_BASE_URL"
# and shows NO snippets. That is measured, not predicted.
#
# ⚠ UNLIKE LENS_BASE_URL, THIS VALUE IS NOT CHECKED AT BOOT. A wrong one starts
# silently and is printed to customers verbatim; the failure lands on their
# machine, not in your log. Set it to the public https origin and verify it from
# outside your network.
LENS_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://REPLACE_ME_LENS_HOST
#
# ⚠ REQUIRED. The BFF exits with "LENS_PROVISION_SECRET is required (…); refusing to
# start" without it (apps/bff/main.go). Same value Lens boots with, under the SAME
# name there (LENS_PROVISION_SECRET in lens.env) — FULL-STACK-DEPLOY.md STEP 1 has the
# digest compare that proves the two agree.
#
# It is deliberately NOT LENS_API_KEY: the admin key authorises EVERY workspace and
# ~30 admin routes, so a BFF compromise would escalate from one tenant to all of them.
# This secret can create a workspace and mint that workspace's session token, nothing
# else. Setting LENS_API_KEY here is ALSO a boot refusal.
LENS_PROVISION_SECRET=REPLACE_ME
#
# ── The two below are INERT, AND KEPT ON PURPOSE ──────────────────────────────
# The current BFF reads NEITHER (grep -c LENS_WORKSPACE apps/bff/main.go = 0). Per-user
# signup (#30) replaced the one pinned workspace: each login is provisioned its own via
# POST /v1/provision, and the credential is a per-session token, not a stored key.
#
# They are not deleted because THE OLD BINARY REQUIRES THEM AND REFUSES TO START
# WITHOUT THEM. A file carrying all three values boots either binary, which is what
# makes the rollback in FULL-STACK-DEPLOY.md a binary swap rather than a binary swap
# plus an env edit under time pressure. The new BFF tolerates them silently — they are
# not in its refusal list (only LENS_API_KEY and TRACK_WORKSPACE_ID are).
#
# Delete them once rollback to a pre-#30 binary is no longer a possibility, and not before.
LENS_WORKSPACE_KEY=tlv_ws_REPLACE_ME
LENS_WORKSPACE_ID=REPLACE_ME

# ── Track (optional PAIR: both or neither; oidc mode only) ───────────────────
# Unset ⇒ /api/track/* and /api/members answer 503; the rest of the app works.
#
# TRACK_WORKSPACE_ID IS GONE, deliberately. Track is per-session now: each signed-in
# person gets their OWN Track workspace, created on first login by Track's idempotent
# POST /v1/bootstrap and read from the session per request. A pinned id here would put
# every trial user back in one shared Track, reading each other's issues — which is the
# defect this replaced. Setting the variable has no effect; the BFF does not read it.
#
# BASE URL — 127.0.0.1, NOT a docker service name (the BFF is a host process and its
# config check refuses anything neither https nor loopback).
#
# THE SECRET IS ONE VALUE UNDER TWO NAMES: TRACK_GATEWAY_SECRET here must equal the
# GATEWAY_AUTH_SECRET Track boots with. A mismatch 401s before Track reads any identity,
# so only /api/track/* breaks — silently, and looking like a Track bug.
# ⚠ TRACK_WORKSPACE_ID NOW REFUSES TO START. It was briefly ignored, which is worse than
# rejected: set, read by nobody, and meaningless — whoever set it believed a pinning that does
# not happen. If you have it in an existing env file, delete the line.
#TRACK_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
#TRACK_GATEWAY_SECRET=

# ── Docs (optional PAIR: both or neither; oidc mode only) ────────────────────
# Unset ⇒ /api/docs/* answers 503.
#
# ⚠ DOCS IS PER-IDENTITY NOW (suite #59, 030ea53). It used to serve ONE workspace shared
# by every signed-in person. Every Docs route now resolves the SESSION's workspace via
# docsWorkspaceFor — which is trackWorkspaceFor — so each person gets their own, exactly
# like Lens and Track.
#
# ⚠ DOCS_WORKSPACE_ID IS GONE. Removed from the BFF entirely; two variables, not three.
# ⚠ AND UNLIKE TRACK_WORKSPACE_ID, LEAVING IT SET IS **SILENTLY IGNORED** — the BFF has no
# refusal for it (only TRACK_WORKSPACE_ID and LENS_API_KEY are rejected). So a stale line
# here states a pinning that does not happen and nothing will tell you. DELETE THE LINE
# from any existing /etc/talyvor/bff.env.
#
# ⚠ DOCS NOW REQUIRES TRACK TO BE CONFIGURED. The workspace id is the one Track mints at
# login, so with Track unset every /api/docs/* route answers
#   503 {"error":"track upstream not configured on this BFF"}
# — a Docs failure that names Track. Configure the Track pair above, or expect this.
#
# The Docs container's DOCS_DEFAULT_WORKSPACE is NOT this variable's counterpart any more:
# it scopes two background jobs (cost sync, daily freshness digest) and pairs with nothing.
#DOCS_BASE_URL=
#DOCS_GATEWAY_SECRET=

# ── Synthetic users (optional; B17.2) ────────────────────────────────────────
# The SAME value as LENS_SYNTHETIC_KEY in lens.env. Set, a headless browser signs in
# one of Lens's synthetic test workspaces with POST /auth/synthetic (header
# X-Talyvor-Synthetic-Key); Lens must report the workspace synthetic, so a real
# account is always refused. Unset (the default), the route answers 404.
#LENS_SYNTHETIC_KEY=

# ── Marketplace review queue (optional; B20.12) ──────────────────────────────
# A moderator key from Lens — it prints one once:
#   docker compose exec lens /lens moderator-keys create web app
# (talyvor-lens docs/moderator-keys.md). With it, anyone on OPERATOR_SUBS sees
# Marketplace → Review queue: approve a reported listing, or take it down with a
# reason and see the buyers' refunds. Each call names the signed-in operator in
# X-Talyvor-Operator. It is NOT a Lens admin key and must never be one. Unset (the
# default), the queue answers 501.
#LENS_MODERATOR_KEY=

# ── Operator screen (optional; B18.25) ───────────────────────────────────────
# The SAME value as LENS_OPERATOR_READ_KEY in Lens's environment. With it, anyone
# on OPERATOR_SUBS sees Operator → Workspaces: every workspace's spend this month
# and all time, the LENS it holds unsettled, and when it last made a request.
# Lens accepts this key for its cross-tenant GETs and nothing else. It is NOT a
# Lens admin key and must never be one. Unset (the default), the screen answers 501.
#LENS_OPERATOR_READ_KEY=
